#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-bundle(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:7
msgid "git-bundle - Move objects and refs by archive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:17
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t'git bundle' create [-q | --quiet | --progress]\n"
"\t\t\t    [--version=<version>] <file> <git-rev-list-args>\n"
"\t'git bundle' verify [-q | --quiet] <file>\n"
"\t'git bundle' list-heads <file> [<refname>...]\n"
"\t'git bundle' unbundle [--progress] <file> [<refname>...]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:24
msgid ""
"Create, unpack, and manipulate \"bundle\" files. Bundles are used for the "
"\"offline\" transfer of Git objects without an active \"server\" sitting on "
"the other side of the network connection."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:29
msgid ""
"They can be used to create both incremental and full backups of a repository "
"(see the \"full backup\" example in \"EXAMPLES\"), and to relay the state of "
"the references in one repository to another (see the second example)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:37
msgid ""
"Git commands that fetch or otherwise \"read\" via protocols such as `ssh://` "
"and `https://` can also operate on bundle files. It is possible "
"linkgit:git-clone[1] a new repository from a bundle, to use "
"linkgit:git-fetch[1] to fetch from one, and to list the references contained "
"within it with linkgit:git-ls-remote[1]. There's no corresponding \"write\" "
"support, i.e. a 'git push' into a bundle is not supported."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "BUNDLE FORMAT"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:43
msgid ""
"Bundles are `.pack` files (see linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]) with a header "
"indicating what references are contained within the bundle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:47
msgid ""
"Like the packed archive format itself bundles can either be self-contained, "
"or be created using exclusions.  See the \"OBJECT PREREQUISITES\" section "
"below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:51
msgid ""
"Bundles created using revision exclusions are \"thin packs\" created using "
"the `--thin` option to linkgit:git-pack-objects[1], and unbundled using the "
"`--fix-thin` option to linkgit:git-index-pack[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:57
msgid ""
"There is no option to create a \"thick pack\" when using revision "
"exclusions, and users should not be concerned about the difference. By using "
"\"thin packs\", bundles created using exclusions are smaller in size. That "
"they're \"thin\" under the hood is merely noted here as a curiosity, and as "
"a reference to other documentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:60
msgid ""
"See linkgit:gitformat-bundle[5] for more details and the discussion of "
"\"thin pack\" in linkgit:gitformat-pack[5] for further details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "create [options] <file> <git-rev-list-args>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:69
msgid ""
"Used to create a bundle named 'file'.  This requires the "
"'<git-rev-list-args>' arguments to define the bundle contents.  'options' "
"contains the options specific to the 'git bundle create' subcommand. If "
"'file' is `-`, the bundle is written to stdout."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "verify <file>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:81
msgid ""
"Used to check that a bundle file is valid and will apply cleanly to the "
"current repository.  This includes checks on the bundle format itself as "
"well as checking that the prerequisite commits exist and are fully linked in "
"the current repository.  Then, 'git bundle' prints a list of missing "
"commits, if any.  Finally, information about additional capabilities, such "
"as \"object filter\", is printed. See \"Capabilities\" in "
"linkgit:gitformat-bundle[5] for more information. The exit code is zero for "
"success, but will be nonzero if the bundle file is invalid. If 'file' is "
"`-`, the bundle is read from stdin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "list-heads <file>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:86
msgid ""
"Lists the references defined in the bundle.  If followed by a list of "
"references, only references matching those given are printed out. If 'file' "
"is `-`, the bundle is read from stdin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "unbundle <file>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:94
msgid ""
"Passes the objects in the bundle to 'git index-pack' for storage in the "
"repository, then prints the names of all defined references. If a list of "
"references is given, only references matching those in the list are "
"printed. This command is really plumbing, intended to be called only by 'git "
"fetch'.  If 'file' is `-`, the bundle is read from stdin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:95
#, no-wrap
msgid "<git-rev-list-args>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:104
msgid ""
"A list of arguments, acceptable to 'git rev-parse' and 'git rev-list' (and "
"containing a named ref, see SPECIFYING REFERENCES below), that specifies the "
"specific objects and references to transport.  For example, "
"`master~10..master` causes the current master reference to be packaged along "
"with all objects added since its 10th ancestor commit.  There is no explicit "
"limit to the number of references and objects that may be packaged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:106
#, no-wrap
msgid "[<refname>...]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:112
msgid ""
"A list of references used to limit the references reported as "
"available. This is principally of use to 'git fetch', which expects to "
"receive only those references asked for and not necessarily everything in "
"the pack (in this case, 'git bundle' acts like 'git fetch-pack')."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:113
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--progress`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:118
msgid ""
"Progress status is reported on the standard error stream by default when it "
"is attached to a terminal, unless -q is specified. This flag forces progress "
"status even if the standard error stream is not directed to a terminal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--version=<version>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:124
msgid ""
"Specify the bundle version.  Version 2 is the older format and can only be "
"used with SHA-1 repositories; the newer version 3 contains capabilities that "
"permit extensions. The default is the oldest supported format, based on the "
"hash algorithm in use."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-q`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:126
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quiet`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:129
msgid ""
"This flag makes the command not to report its progress on the standard error "
"stream."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "SPECIFYING REFERENCES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:135
msgid ""
"Revisions must be accompanied by reference names to be packaged in a "
"bundle.  Alternatively `--all` can be used to package all refs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:139
msgid ""
"More than one reference may be packaged, and more than one set of "
"prerequisite objects can be specified.  The objects packaged are those not "
"contained in the union of the prerequisites."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:144
msgid ""
"The 'git bundle create' command resolves the reference names for you using "
"the same rules as `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref=loose`. Each prerequisite can "
"be specified explicitly (e.g. `^master~10`), or implicitly "
"(e.g. `master~10..master`, `--since=10.days.ago master`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:147
msgid ""
"All of these simple cases are OK (assuming we have a \"master\" and \"next\" "
"branch):"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:153
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git bundle create master.bundle master\n"
"$ echo master | git bundle create master.bundle --stdin\n"
"$ git bundle create master-and-next.bundle master next\n"
"$ (echo master; echo next) | git bundle create master-and-next.bundle "
"--stdin\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:156
msgid "And so are these (and the same but omitted `--stdin` examples):"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:160
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git bundle create recent-master.bundle master~10..master\n"
"$ git bundle create recent-updates.bundle master~10..master next~5..next\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:164
msgid ""
"A revision name or a range whose right-hand-side cannot be resolved to a "
"reference is not accepted:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:170
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git bundle create HEAD.bundle $(git rev-parse HEAD)\n"
"fatal: Refusing to create empty bundle.\n"
"$ git bundle create master-yesterday.bundle master~10..master~5\n"
"fatal: Refusing to create empty bundle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "OBJECT PREREQUISITES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:179
msgid ""
"When creating bundles it is possible to create a self-contained bundle that "
"can be unbundled in a repository with no common history, as well as "
"providing negative revisions to exclude objects needed in the earlier parts "
"of the history."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:184
msgid ""
"Feeding a revision such as `new` to `git bundle create` will create a bundle "
"file that contains all the objects reachable from the revision `new`. That "
"bundle can be unbundled in any repository to obtain a full history that "
"leads to the revision `new`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:187
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git bundle create full.bundle new\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:192
msgid ""
"A revision range such as `old..new` will produce a bundle file that will "
"require the revision `old` (and any objects reachable from it)  to exist for "
"the bundle to be \"unbundle\"-able:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git bundle create full.bundle old..new\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:200
msgid ""
"A self-contained bundle without any prerequisites can be extracted into "
"anywhere, even into an empty repository, or be cloned from (i.e., `new`, but "
"not `old..new`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:204
msgid ""
"It is okay to err on the side of caution, causing the bundle file to contain "
"objects already in the destination, as these are ignored when unpacking at "
"the destination."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:208
msgid ""
"If you want to provide the same set of refs that a clone directly from the "
"source repository would get, use `--branches --tags` for the "
"`<git-rev-list-args>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:212
msgid ""
"The 'git bundle verify' command can be used to check whether your recipient "
"repository has the required prerequisite commits for a bundle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:214
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:217
msgid "We'll discuss two cases:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:219
msgid "Taking a full backup of a repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:221
msgid ""
"Transferring the history of a repository to another machine when the two "
"machines have no direct connection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:225
msgid ""
"First let's consider a full backup of the repository.  The following command "
"will take a full backup of the repository in the sense that all refs are "
"included in the bundle:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:228
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git bundle create backup.bundle --all\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:234
msgid ""
"But note again that this is only for the refs, i.e. you will only include "
"refs and commits reachable from those refs.  You will not include other "
"local state, such as the contents of the index, working tree, the stash, "
"per-repository configuration, hooks, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:237
msgid ""
"You can later recover that repository by using for example "
"linkgit:git-clone[1]:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:240
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git clone backup.bundle <new directory>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:247
msgid ""
"For the next example, assume you want to transfer the history from a "
"repository R1 on machine A to another repository R2 on machine B.  For "
"whatever reason, direct connection between A and B is not allowed, but we "
"can move data from A to B via some mechanism (CD, email, etc.).  We want to "
"update R2 with development made on the branch master in R1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:252
msgid ""
"To bootstrap the process, you can first create a bundle that does not have "
"any prerequisites. You can use a tag to remember up to what commit you last "
"processed, in order to make it easy to later update the other repository "
"with an incremental bundle:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:257
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"machineA$ cd R1\n"
"machineA$ git bundle create file.bundle master\n"
"machineA$ git tag -f lastR2bundle master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:262
msgid ""
"Then you transfer file.bundle to the target machine B. Because this bundle "
"does not require any existing object to be extracted, you can create a new "
"repository on machine B by cloning from it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:265
#, no-wrap
msgid "machineB$ git clone -b master /home/me/tmp/file.bundle R2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:270
msgid ""
"This will define a remote called \"origin\" in the resulting repository that "
"lets you fetch and pull from the bundle. The $GIT_DIR/config file in R2 will "
"have an entry like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:275
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[remote \"origin\"]\n"
"    url = /home/me/tmp/file.bundle\n"
"    fetch = refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:280
msgid ""
"To update the resulting mine.git repository, you can fetch or pull after "
"replacing the bundle stored at /home/me/tmp/file.bundle with incremental "
"updates."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:283
msgid ""
"After working some more in the original repository, you can create an "
"incremental bundle to update the other repository:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:288
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"machineA$ cd R1\n"
"machineA$ git bundle create file.bundle lastR2bundle..master\n"
"machineA$ git tag -f lastR2bundle master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:292
msgid ""
"You then transfer the bundle to the other machine to replace "
"/home/me/tmp/file.bundle, and pull from it."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:296
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"machineB$ cd R2\n"
"machineB$ git pull\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:304
msgid ""
"If you know up to what commit the intended recipient repository should have "
"the necessary objects, you can use that knowledge to specify the "
"prerequisites, giving a cut-off point to limit the revisions and objects "
"that go in the resulting bundle. The previous example used the lastR2bundle "
"tag for this purpose, but you can use any other options that you would give "
"to the linkgit:git-log[1] command. Here are more examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:306
msgid "You can use a tag that is present in both:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:309
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git bundle create mybundle v1.0.0..master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:312
msgid "You can use a prerequisite based on time:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:315
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git bundle create mybundle --since=10.days master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:318
msgid "You can use the number of commits:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:321
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git bundle create mybundle -10 master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:325
msgid ""
"You can run `git-bundle verify` to see if you can extract from a bundle that "
"was created with a prerequisite:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:328
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git bundle verify mybundle\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:332
msgid ""
"This will list what commits you must have in order to extract from the "
"bundle and will error out if you do not have them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:336
msgid ""
"A bundle from a recipient repository's point of view is just like a regular "
"repository which it fetches or pulls from. You can, for example, map "
"references when fetching:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:339
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git fetch mybundle master:localRef\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:342
msgid "You can also see what references it offers:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:345
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git ls-remote mybundle\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:348
#, no-wrap
msgid "DISCUSSION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:354
msgid ""
"A naive way to make a full backup of a repository is to use something to the "
"effect of `cp -r <repo> <destination>`.  This is discouraged since the "
"repository could be written to during the copy operation.  In turn some "
"files at `<destination>` could be corrupted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:361
msgid ""
"This is why it is recommended to use Git tooling for making repository "
"backups, either with this command or with e.g. linkgit:git-clone[1].  But "
"keep in mind that these tools will not help you backup state other than refs "
"and commits.  In other words they will not help you backup contents of the "
"index, working tree, the stash, per-repository configuration, hooks, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:364
msgid ""
"See also linkgit:gitfaq[7], section \"TRANSFERS\" for a discussion of the "
"problems associated with file syncing across systems."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:366
#, no-wrap
msgid "FILE FORMAT"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:369
msgid "See linkgit:gitformat-bundle[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:371
#, no-wrap
msgid "GIT"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-bundle.adoc:372
msgid "Part of the linkgit:git[1] suite"
msgstr ""
